+    #
+    # Upstream filters `Status != "dead"`, which hides two very different
+    # things: a deployment the user deleted, and a deployment that died by
+    # itself. The second is the whole reason someone opens this page.
+    #
+    # Observed: a module deployed with the `jupyter` service goes
+    # starting -> running -> gone, with no error anywhere in the interface,
+    # because its container exits 1 and Nomad marks the job dead after two
+    # restarts. Three of them accumulated before anyone knew why.
+    #
+    # Nomad's `Stop` field separates the cases exactly: it is true only when
+    # something asked the job to stop. So `dead and Stop == false` is
+    # "it failed", and that is what a user needs to see.
+    #
+    # Same principle as D-39 and D-50, applied to the other end of a
+    # deployment's life: "deleted" and "crashed" are different words, and
+    # showing neither is the worst of the three options.
     job_filter = (
